! mcp_bootstrap_p190.strap -- MCP printed page 190: the WRITE-TAPE-MARK
! RETRY arm of the tape error routine, and the DENSITY CHECK subroutine
! that follows it.
!
! docs/MCP_Mar63_001-249.pdf PDF p.197.  The header was READ, not
! assumed, at 200 and 600 dpi:  "TIME CLOCK 00054041   PUNID 11D11MCP
! CARD ID LG000076   DIST. OF 3/01   3/01   PAGE NUMBER  190".
!
! >>> THE COLUMN-HEADING ROW'S OWN "LOCATION" FIELD IS BLANK. <<<
! Cropped at 600 dpi (x 7200, W 3400) and looked at directly: the
! word LOCATION, then clean paper to the right-hand rule.  NO DIGITS
! ARE RECORDED HERE, because the dashed rule runs through that field
! and tesseract invents numbers out of it (workflow step 1).  A blank
! is what the rule PREDICTS for this page: the field is LINE 1's
! location and line 1 here is an asterisk banner card that compiles
! nothing.  It is therefore NOT a witness for this page's origin, and
! this page has no other independent witness either -- see THE JOIN.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT THE PAGE IS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! FIFTY-THREE PRINTED LINES, of which FORTY-THREE carry a location
! and compile.  Ten compile nothing: lines 1-3 and 36-42, which are
! comment cards (they print a "-" in the NAME column and no location
! and no binary at all).  The page is CONTIGUOUS: its FIRST located
! row is line 4 at 221444.40 and its LAST is line 53 at 221474.00;
! the counter LEAVES the page at 221474.40.  Thirty-eight half-word
! rows and five full-word rows is forty-eight half words, EXACTLY
! TWENTY-FOUR WORDS, which is exactly 221474.40 minus 221444.40.
! NO reservation, NO SLC, NO VF, NO DD and NO hole of any kind; the
! counter chain was walked row by row off the LOCATION column and
! never jumps.
!
!   lines  1-3    banner: "RETRY FOR WRITE TAPE MARK OP"
!   lines  4-18   K R WEF: rewind the tape (control code 0o136),
!                 wait for the channel, clear the control word,
!                 set installation density, then go to J R WRIT
!                 to erase a long gap and rewrite the label.
!   lines 19-26   the file-density arm: load S F DEN, set the unit
!                 to it, re-issue the Write Tape Mark (0o117).
!   lines 27-35   the termination test, the three-tries give-up
!                 (RK2 / K SOS TM) and the successful exit.
!   lines 36-42   banner and CALLING SEQUENCE for the next routine.
!   lines 43-53   K K DENS, the density-check subroutine: compare
!                 the wanted density against S U DENS, pick the
!                 0o36 / 0o37 control code, set the UST bit, and
!                 re-issue the control word.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> THIS PAGE PRINTS FOUR 729 IV CONTROL CODES AS LITERALS, WITH
! >>> THE OPERATOR'S ENGLISH BESIDE THEM.  READ THE LIMITS ON THIS
! >>> BEFORE QUOTING IT: IT IS NOT THE LEG SPFL AND REW MET. <<<
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The page never spells an I-O function pseudo-op.  It loads the
! control code into index 2 as an IMMEDIATE and lets a later CTL
! use it, so the code itself is printed in the BINARY OUTPUT column
! with the operator's own English two columns away:
!
!   line  5   LVI,2,(8)136.   000136.05 01   -REWIND TAPE
!   line 23   LVI,2,(8)117.   000117.05 01   -RETRY WEF OP
!   line 47   LVI,2,(8)36.    000036.05 01   -HIGH DENSITY DESIRED
!   line 49   LVI,2,(8)37.    000037.05 01   -LOW DENSITY DESIRED
!
! docs/exchange_and_io.md's transcription of Reference Manual p.132
! gives 036 High Density Mode, 037 Low Density Mode, 117 Write Tape
! Mark, 136 Rewind.  0o136 CORROBORATES the already-implemented REW
! (printed p.165 was its listing leg; this is a second, independent
! one, and this page's English says "REWIND TAPE" in as many words).
!
! >>> BE EXACT ABOUT WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT. <<<  strap_asm's
! IO_FUNCTION_OPS note admits a mnemonic on three legs, and the
! listing leg it names is specific: SPFL came from printed p.3 line
! 35 printing 000077 IN A CTL'S SECOND ADDRESS FIELD with .15 =
! CTLSEOP in bits 50-55, and REW from printed p.165 line 11 printing
! 000136 THE SAME WAY.  MY FOUR ROWS ARE NOT THAT.  They are LVI
! immediates: the code lands in an index register's value field,
! there is no CTL on this page, no channel operand and no CTLSEOP.
! What I measured is that the code appears in MCP beside the
! operator's English, which CORROBORATES but is a weaker leg.  What
! would finish it for WEF: any page printing 000117 with .15 in bits
! 50-55, the way p.3 and p.165 do.
!
! WHAT THIS PAGE DOES CLOSE IS THE *NAME* GAP FOR "WEF", AND THAT IS
! THE PART WORTH CARRYING.  The note refuses a mnemonic whose
! Appendix B NAME only resembles the p.132 table's name -- "Write
! End-of-File" against "Write Tape Mark" is exactly that kind of
! near-match, the "Backspace" against "Backspace Block" case.  THIS
! PAGE IS THE DOCUMENT THAT JOINS THE TWO NAMES: its banner reads
! "RETRY FOR WRITE TAPE MARK OP", the routine it names is K R WEF,
! and line 23's comment calls 0o117 "RETRY WEF OP".  IBM's own
! programmer writes WEF for the operation the Reference Manual calls
! Write Tape Mark.  Name gap closed; code leg still the weaker kind.
!
! AND HD/LD DO *NOT* GET THE SAME TREATMENT -- a first draft of this
! deck said they did and that was wrong.  Appendix B says "High
! Density" and "Low Density"; the p.132 table says "High Density
! MODE" and "Low Density MODE".  That is the near-match the note
! rejects, and these rows are LVI immediates besides.  I am NOT
! implementing any of these (scope); I am recording the evidence and
! its exact weight.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE SURVEY, AND THE SECOND MACHINE READING
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE CACHED SURVEY IS KEYED BY *PDF* PAGE, NOT PRINTED PAGE.
! docs/mcp_page_map.json's "190" is PDF 190 = printed 183, and
! quoting it for this page would be an error of a whole different
! kind.  The entry for printed p.190 is "197":
!
!   rows 27   first 221444.64   last 221465.40
!
! ALL THREE DISAGREE WITH THE PAGE, AND THE PAGE WINS ALL THREE:
!
!  * first 221444.64 is not a floor, it is IMPOSSIBLE.  A STRAP
!    instruction starts on a half word, so a location's fraction is
!    .00 or .40 and never .64.  The page prints 221444.40, read at
!    600 dpi, and every one of the other forty-two located rows
!    follows from it by the counter chain with no gap.
!  * last 221465.40 is line 35, EIGHTEEN ROWS SHORT of line 53's
!    221474.00 -- and it is exactly where mcp_ocr.py stops, which is
!    the brief's diagnosis reproduced on a fresh page.
!  * rows 27 against 43 located rows out of 53 printed lines.
!
! THE SECOND MACHINE READING AGREES WHERE IT CAN SEE (workflow step
! 2a).  "python3 strap/mcp_ocr.py 190" reads 17 locations:
! 221444.40, 221445.00, 221446.40, 221447.00, 221450.00, 221450.40,
! 221452.00, 221452.40, 221454.40, 221455.40, 221456.40, 221457.00,
! 221457.40, 221460.40, 221461.00, 221461.40, 221465.40.  EVERY ONE
! is in this deck at the row the deck puts it in; ZERO disagreements.
! It then gives up.  So there is no row the OCR read that I dispute
! -- it simply stops, exactly as the floors rule predicts.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE JOIN -- THE ORIGIN IS ASSERTED, NOT CHECKED
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! The page prints NO "SLC,$", so the deck has to write a hard
! origin, and a hard SLC cannot disagree with an arriving counter
! (workflow, WHAT THE ORACLE CANNOT SEE #2).  The way out is the
! merge: if a landed deck SYNs a label this page DEFINES, the merge
! compares them and the origin becomes CHECKED.  I ran that query.
! THIS PAGE DEFINES FIVE LABELS -- K R WEF, RK2, K SOS TM, K K DENS
! and K LOW -- AND NO LANDED DECK REFERENCES ANY OF THEM:
!
!   $ cd decks && grep -n 'KRWEF\|RK2\|KSOSTM\|KKDENS\|KLOW' *.strap
!   (no output)
!
! AND I RAN THE INJECTION ANYWAY RATHER THAN ARGUING FROM THE GREP.
! Merging printed pp.99-184 plus this page, with the origin moved one
! half word to (8)221445.00:
!
!   origin as read       -> merge OK, 771 defined, 78 external
!   origin shifted .40   -> merge OK, 771 defined        <- ACCEPTED
!
! The merge does NOT refuse it.  That is the measurement, and it says
! ASSERTED outright instead of leaving it to inference.
!
! So I must say ASSERTED.  What the origin rests on is line 4's own
! printed LOCATION, 221444.40, read at 600 dpi, and the fact that
! every one of the other forty-two located rows follows from it by
! the counter chain with no gap -- which is a strong internal check
! of the SPAN and no check at all of where the span starts.
!
! FALSIFIABLE PREDICTION FOR printed p.189: its last located row is
! at 221444.00 and is a HALF WORD, so that p.190 line 4 lands on
! 221444.40.  If p.189 ends on a full word at 221443.40 or on
! 221444.40 itself, one of us is wrong and this origin is the thing
! to doubt first.
!
! FALSIFIABLE PREDICTION FOR printed p.191: its first located row is
! 221474.40, and it is K DEN OK -- this page SICs that label THREE
! times (lines 14, 21 and 52's pair) and an SIC reads its operand to
! the bit, so 221474.40 is a BIT-EXACT reading, not a convention.
! p.191 should also define K CTL CM at 221475.00, K K EOP at
! 221476.00 and K SEOP R at 221502.00, in that order, because this
! page reads all four and they are consecutive.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! >>> ONE SUBSTITUTION, AND IT IS LOUD <<<
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! LINE 34 PRINTS      K SOS TM  CB,2,K R WEF.32
! THIS DECK WRITES    KSOSTM    CB,2,KRWEF.32
!
! strap_asm REFUSES the printed form -- "line NN: undefined symbol
! 'K'" -- because a blank inside a name INSIDE AN EXPRESSION is not
! parsed, which is the known gap the brief lists ("S TLR+14.",
! "K GOOF+.32").  The bare name "K R WEF" resolves perfectly; only
! the ".32" suffix breaks it.  The substitution closes up the blanks
! in the NAME ONLY and changes no value: KRWEF is this page's own
! line 4 at 221444.40, +32 bits is 221445.00, and the row's !CHECK
! below is the PRINTED 221445.04 48, so the check still tests the
! address this page prints.  Every other row in this deck is the
! printed form verbatim.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE LOCATION-COLUMN ASTERISK
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! TWO ROWS CARRY IT: line 6 (221445.40, LX,0,K CTL CM) and line 27
! (221461.00, LVI,1,$+1.0).  I am recording it and proposing no
! rule, as the brief requires.  What it is NOT, on this page: it is
! not the mnemonic (lines 24 and 51 are the SAME LX card with no
! asterisk), it is not "$-relative" (line 48's B,$+1.0 has none), it
! is not a label row (neither is named), it is not a branch target
! inside this page, and it is NOT "the first statement of a
! two-statement card": line 27 does share serial LG000095 with line
! 28, but line 6 is ALONE on LG000081, and the eight SIC rows that
! ARE the first statement of a two-statement card carry no asterisk.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! CARD SERIALS -- AND WHAT THEY SHOW ABOUT TWO-STATEMENT CARDS
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! LG000076 on line 1 (which is also the header's CARD ID) through
! LG000118 on line 53.  Forty-three distinct serials over fifty-three
! lines, and the repeats are not errors: TEN SERIALS APPEAR TWICE --
! 82 (lines 7-8), 83 (9-10), 84 (11-12), 86 (14-15), 88 (17-18),
! 91 (21-22), 94 (25-26), 95 (27-28), 101 (34-35) and 118 (52-53).
! EIGHT of the ten are an SIC and its branch; the other two are
! LVI/branch (95) and CB/branch (101), so the pairing is "two
! statements on one card", not "an SIC pair".  Line 41 prints the
! reason in plain sight --
! it is a COMMENT CARD showing the calling sequence as
! "SIC,K DEN OK<sep>B,K K DENS" on ONE card, with STRAP's statement
! separator between them.  So one card can hold two statements and
! the listing prints them on two lines under one serial.  Nothing
! fell off either end: the first serial equals the header's CARD ID
! and the last two lines share LG000118.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT I READ OFF THE INK AND WHAT I DID NOT
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! This page drops ink on round glyphs constantly: line 20 prints its
! octal fraction field as ".C2", line 17 prints "221660.CC", line 26
! prints its hex byte as "CO".  Those are octal/branch fields that
! CANNOT hold a C, so they are 0s with certainty (workflow rung 5) --
! and per the same note that licenses NOTHING about the letters.
! Every round glyph in a NAME on this page was settled by a TYPESET
! manual or a landed deck, never by shape:
!
!   KKEOP   5 hits in C22-6728/6678, KKECP 0   -> "K K EOP"
!   KIOREJ  7 hits (KICREJ 3, KIGREJ 2)        -> "K IO REJ"
!   SEOP    134 hits (SECP 15, and JSEOPT 5
!           against JSECPT 1)                  -> "SEOP", "J SEOP T"
!   KRWEF   5 hits                             -> "K R WEF"
!   KREP    5 hits, and landed as SYN, 30      -> "K REP"
!   KKDENS  5 hits                             -> "K K DENS"
!   JRWRIT  4 hits                             -> "J R WRIT"
!   SFDEN   2 hits, and landed SYN(BU,2), 2.27 -> "S F DEN"
!   RK2     2 hits                             -> "RK2"
!   KSOS    landed by printed p.125 at 221274.00
!   KCLEAR / KCBJR / KUNRR / KKLOOP / SINSTD / SUDENS all landed
!
! "K LOW" is settled by its own row's ENGLISH (rung 1): line 49's
! comment is "-LOW DENSITY DESIRED" and the label and the comment
! print the same word on the same row.
!
! THE R IN "K SEOP R" WAS READ TWICE AT 1200 dpi -- ON LINE 7 AND
! AGAIN ON LINE 52, which are the SAME CARD printed twice on one
! page in the same character position.  That is rung 4, the
! strongest rung in the ladder, and both instances carry the R's
! diagonal leg.  IT MATTERS THAT BOTH WERE READ, because the typeset
! manuals print KSEOPT twenty-three times and KSEOPR never: the
! manual would have talked a single reading out of being right.
! R/T is not one of this drum's confusable pairs and neither glyph
! is ambiguous.  If a later page DEFINES 221502.00 and spells it
! with a T, that page wins and this note is the thing to re-read.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! WHAT I COULD NOT SETTLE
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! 1. "K SOS" / "K SOS TM".  I inherited the O from printed p.125's
!    landed KSOS and the merge will keep us consistent, but NOBODY
!    has settled it independently: the typeset manuals contain
!    neither KSOS nor KSCS, the volume OCR splits 12 "K SOS" to 10
!    "K SCS" (which is not a vote -- same drum, same reader), and
!    shape cannot separate O from C on this drum.  WHAT WOULD SETTLE
!    IT: printed p.187-ish, wherever 221274.00 is DEFINED, with a
!    comment beside it; or any typeset prose naming the routine.
! 2. "K CTL CM", "K DEN OK", "K K RET", "K K EXIT", "J PREV X" and
!    "J WRIT X" have no typeset hit at all.  Their round glyphs are
!    read as O's from the English they plainly spell (CONTROL,
!    DENSITY OK, PREVIOUS, WRITE), which is an argument and not a
!    reading.  Each is defined on a page nobody has opened; the page
!    that DEFINES it is the authority and should overrule this deck.
! 3. THE ASTERISK.  See above.  Two instances, no rule.
! 4. The bit offsets marked "half word only" below are NOT readings.
!    Measured on this page's own operations (sweep over a SYN at
!    .00/.01/.37/.40/.41): TI, LX, LVI and BZB all collapse every
!    offset below 32 to one printed word, and LCI collapses ALL
!    SIXTY-FOUR -- "LCI,2,X" prints the same 000036.05 02 for X at
!    30.00 and at 30.77, so K REP is pinned to a WORD and not even
!    to a half word.  The SIC and VFL rows are the opposite case and
!    are bit-exact; they are marked so, one by one.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------
! THE EXTERNALS, AND EXACTLY HOW WELL EACH IS KNOWN
! ---------------------------------------------------------------

KREP    SYN(BU,18), 30              ! dds (BU,18) from printed p.185 line 27,
